  "analysis_insights": {
    "question_1_system1_vs_system2": "System-1 Decision models (/api/alpha/decisions, e.g., JEV, Luna, CLEF) operate at ultra-low latencies (30\u2013120ms/move), winning speed races on direct paths but occasionally exhibiting suboptimal wandering or local loops. In contrast, Frontier LLMs (Claude Opus 5.5) represent System-2 deliberate reasoning: higher per-move latency (~900\u20131400ms) but superior move efficiency and spatial goal-directedness on deeper scrambles.",
    "question_2_optimality_gap_and_success_rate": "Evaluating models solely on Optimality Gap (Model moves - Exact shortest moves) suffers from survivorship bias because it conditions exclusively on solved trials. A fragile model solving 1 out of 5 puzzles in 4 moves (Gap = 0) appears mathematically optimal while failing 80% of tasks. A robust model solving 5 out of 5 in 6 moves (Gap = 2) provides dramatically higher utility. Optimality Gap must always be paired with Success Rate.",
    "question_3_malformed_response_rates": "Decision models exhibit near-zero malformed responses because /api/alpha/decisions treats move selection as a discrete logit-masked classification over predefined candidate criteria. In contrast, /api/v1/chat/completions is an unconstrained autoregressive token generator that can suffer from schema drift, markdown wrapping, or hallucinated move notation (e.g., 'R3', 'Right') under spatial ambiguity.",
    "question_4_exact_bfs_scalability": "Exact BFS shortest-path solving scales as O(18 * 15^(d-1)). For primary benchmark scrambles (3\u20135 moves), bidirectional BFS explores under 10,000 states in < 25ms. However, at 15\u201320 moves, the state space explodes to > 10^17 states, exceeding available physical RAM. For extension tiers, exact BFS must be supplemented with Kociemba's Two-Phase or IDA* heuristic pattern databases.",
    "question_5_robotics_deployment_recommendations": "For practical real-time robotic systems (e.g., robotic arm manipulation or automated speedcubing), a hierarchical hybrid architecture is optimal: deploy fast Decision models for high-frequency reactive execution (System-1), with fallback to a deliberative Frontier LLM or heuristic solver (System-2) only when state cycle detection triggers or progress stalls."
